  "text": "Science & Exploration Cheops Characterising exoplanets known to be orbiting around nearby bright stars The mission Cheops is ESA's CH aracterising E x OP lanet S atellite. It is the first space mission dedicated to studying bright, nearby stars that are already known to host exoplanets, in order to make high-precision observations of the planet's size as it passes in front of its host star. It focuses on planets in the super-Earth to Neptune size range, with its data enabling the bulk density of the planets to be derived – a first-step characterisation towards understanding these alien worlds. The launch Launch date: 18 December 2019 Launch site: Europe's Spaceport in French Guiana Launch vehicle: Soyuz-Fregat Orbit: Sun-synchronous, dusk-dawn orbit at 700 km above Earth 1.5 m size 280 kg mass 19×19 arcmin field of view 1.2 Gbit/day data Latest All Stories Videos Images Story Science & Exploration Cheops discovers late bloomer from another era 12/02/2026 5906 views 43 likes Read Story Science & Exploration Clingy planets can trigger own doom, suspect Cheops and TESS 02/07/2025 5272 views 57 likes Read Story Science & Exploration First ‘glory’ on hellish distant world? 05/04/2024 9253 views 36 likes Read Story Science & Exploration ESA’s Cheops helps unlock rare six-planet system 29/11/2023 10777 views 138 likes Read Story Science & Exploration Cheops shows scorching exoplanet acts like a mirror 10/07/2023 25452 views 121 likes Read Image Science & Exploration Cheops explores mysterious warm mini-Neptunes 08/06/2023 6625 views 105 likes View Video 00:00:57 Science & Exploration ESA’s exoplanet missions 20/03/2023 8131 views 182 likes Play Image Science & Exploration Artist impression of planet WASP-103b and its host star 11/01/2022 4601 views 41 likes View Image Science & Exploration Cheops reveals a rugby ball-shaped exoplanet 11/01/2022 4133 views 29 likes View Image Science & Exploration ESA’s new and future exoplanet missions 07/12/2021 12048 views 133